  "prompt_text": "The figure compares three conceptual ways a bioresponsive material can release a payload in biological settings. In all cases, a particle-like carrier contains many small molecules and then encounters an external environment where release occurs, but the trigger and control differ.\n\nIn the directly activated model, the material enters a biological environment that immediately causes a release event, shown by many payload molecules dispersing outward. This represents a simple one-step response to the surroundings.\n\nThe progressively activated model adds an intermediate step. The carrier includes an “activatable motif,” and exposure to a first environment appears to activate or uncover this feature without yet fully releasing the contents. Only after moving into a second environment does substantial release occur. This illustrates staged activation followed by payload delivery.\n\nThe self-regulated model shows a material that first releases cargo in its original environment, then transitions into a “regulated environment” where release is reduced or more controlled. A curved arrow suggests feedback between the changed environment and the material’s behavior. Overall, the figure emphasizes increasing levels of sophistication: immediate triggering, sequential activation, and adaptive self-regulation of release.",
